# .memory-tree.conf — per-repo config for the memory-tree hygiene kit.
# Copy to your repo ROOT as `.memory-tree.conf` and EDIT. Sourced by every kit script
# (check-memory-hygiene.sh, gen_build_index.py, corpus_ids.py, gotchas.py, adopt-memory-tree.sh).
# Plain shell assignments only.

# Root folder of the structured memory tree, relative to the repo root.
MEMORY_ROOT=memory

# DISCIPLINES is a CLOSED ENUM of stream values, NOT a list of directories. The tree is FLAT: every
# build folder sits directly at $MEMORY_ROOT/builds/<slug>/ with no discipline segment and no date
# prefix, and which stream a spec served is declared in its own status header as
# `· streams <value>[+<value>]`. Space-separated, lowercase, [a-z-] only. Add a value only when
# content exists for it. REPLACE the example below with your project's streams.
DISCIPLINES="architecture deployment design"

# <enum value>:FAMILY — the id prefix for that stream's decisions (FAMILY-<slug>-<seq>), its backlog
# shard at $MEMORY_ROOT/backlog/<FAMILY>.md, and the optional FAMILY qualifier in a recording
# filename. There is ONE append-only $MEMORY_ROOT/DECISIONS.md for every family, grouped for
# reading. Space-separated, FAMILY uppercase [A-Z].
FAMILIES="architecture:ARCH deployment:DEPL design:DES"

# Old tree roots being migrated away from; blank when scaffolding fresh.
TOMBSTONE_ROOTS=""

# Spec-format ratchet (check 12). Set to the date you adopt the kit: every spec whose FILENAME date
# is >= this must follow $MEMORY_ROOT/TEMPLATE-SPEC.md — the status header, and for Tier-2 the ten
# canonical ## sections with no empty bodies. Older specs are grandfathered by filename date; never
# retrofit them. Blank = check 12 off. (§10 Reuse audit is separately date-gated by SPEC10_CUTOFF,
# which is a script default in check-memory-hygiene.sh and overridable from the environment.)
SPEC_FORMAT_CUTOFF=""

# Streams ratchet (check 12): specs dated >= this MUST carry `· streams <value>` in the status
# header. Set it strictly AHEAD of every committed spec's filename date at adoption, so no landed
# spec goes retroactively red — which also means your corpus does not exercise the required arm, and
# the kit's self-test carries explicit red/green fixtures for it instead.
STREAMS_CUTOFF=""
# Acceptance-witness ratchet (check 12): specs dated >= this must give every acceptance bullet a
# backticked token. Set it AHEAD of your corpus so nothing lands retroactively red. Blank = off.
SPEC_WITNESS_CUTOFF=""
# The fourth cutoff, and the only one that ships WITH a value: it SELECTS between two spec section
# canons rather than switching a rule on, so check 12 must pick one for every spec. Blank resolves
# FORWARD to the kit's date rather than turning anything off. Raise it to grandfather more of your
# corpus; lower it to ratchet an existing corpus toward the ten-section canon.
SPEC10_CUTOFF="2026-08-04"

# The FIFTH cutoff, and it takes the semantics of the three RULE cutoffs rather than SPEC10_CUTOFF's:
# blank means OFF. A Tier-2 spec dated >= this must RECORD its reuse audit in §10 — the recall terms
# used, AND the probe result (a `reuse_lookup` citation, an explicit "no existing seam fits", or a
# named `reuse-first` waiver). Without it, check 12 grades §10 on presence and non-emptiness alone,
# so "N/A — none" is a passing reuse audit and BUILD-METHOD M7's regrounding step 5 has no terms to
# re-run. Set it STRICTLY AHEAD of every dated spec on every live branch, not just your own: a
# cutoff on today's date reds this leg on the default branch for every in-flight branch carrying a
# spec dated today. Your corpus then exercises neither arm, which is why the kit self-test carries
# red and green fixtures for both.
SPEC10_EVIDENCE_CUTOFF=""

# Rev-scope ratchet (check 12): from this date, a §9 revision entry numbered rev-2 or higher must
# name what it MOVED — at least one `§<n>`, `S<n>` or `AC<n>` token anywhere in the entry, with
# continuation lines folded in. rev-1 is exempt, because a first draft moved everything. Blank =
# never required. Set it STRICTLY AHEAD of every dated spec on every live branch AND ahead of a date
# your fleet can still write into, as with the cutoffs above; your corpus then exercises neither arm,
# which is why the kit self-test carries red and green fixtures for both. The engine prints a
# zero-population notice while that is true, so the silence is announced rather than assumed.
REV_SCOPE_CUTOFF=""

# Scope-join ratchet (check 12): from this date, every column-0 item in a spec's `## N. Scope (IN)`
# must name at least one acceptance criterion — `AC` followed by digits — or carry the marker
# `NOT OBSERVED` and the reason none observes it. One escape spelling and no synonyms, because a
# false red names its own remedy while a false pass is silent. The arm needs BOTH the Scope and the
# Acceptance heading present, matched by heading TEXT rather than by ordinal, so a Tier-1 spec that
# legitimately writes no acceptance section is untouched. Blank = never required. Set it STRICTLY
# AHEAD of every dated spec on every live branch, as with the cutoffs above; the engine prints a
# zero-population notice for as long as that leaves the arm grading nothing.
SCOPE_JOIN_CUTOFF=""

# Failure-mode ratchet (check 12): from this date, every numbered acceptance bullet must name the
# break that would turn it red, in a clause marked `Red when:`, matched case-insensitively as a
# substring so it may sit on a continuation line. No `N/A` and no second form. It reads THIS key and
# no other — the per-bullet walk it rides is hoisted out of the acceptance-witness guard, so arming
# this key while SPEC_WITNESS_CUTOFF stays blank gives you a live arm rather than a silent one.
# Blank = never required; set it strictly ahead of your corpus as with the cutoffs above.
SPEC_FAILURE_MODE_CUTOFF=""

# Two BRANCHES of check 23 (the acceptance ledger), never independent checks: a blank
# ACCEPTANCE_LEDGER_CUTOFF disarms both whatever these say. LEDGER_LABEL_CUTOFF walks the LEDGER
# side and reds an answer whose criterion label the spec does not number — every other arm walks the
# spec side, so that direction was unwatched. LEDGER_TOKEN_CUTOFF requires an answer to share a
# backticked token with the criterion it answers, so the two are joined by content rather than by
# label alone. Case-folded containment either way, and an empty list on either side is silent.
# Blank = off for each; set them strictly ahead of your corpus as with the cutoffs above.
LEDGER_LABEL_CUTOFF=""
# Its own block, not a shared one: the contiguous comment lines directly above a key row are what
# a reader and the gate both take as that key's documentation. Same rule — a BRANCH of check 23,
# so a blank ACCEPTANCE_LEDGER_CUTOFF disarms it whatever this key says.
LEDGER_TOKEN_CUTOFF=""

# Sibling-edge ratchet (check 12): from this date a Tier-2 spec carries a `### Edges` sub-head inside
# §3, holding one bullet per edge or the single word `none` — `**consumes-from**` or `**hands-off**`,
# then a backticked sibling unit id or the bare word `external`. The SHAPE arm reads one spec and
# runs under `--staged` too; the reciprocity, order and payload JOINS are held there and say so,
# because the staged selection would report one end of a correct pair as missing. A join whose target
# is outside the graded population is silent by design, not passing. Blank = never required; set it
# strictly ahead of your corpus as with the cutoffs above.
SPEC_EDGES_CUTOFF=""

# The §5 production-readiness row LABELS, in skeleton order, `|`-separated. This is the ONLY literal
# row set — the spec template renders it through `{{READINESS_ROWS}}` and no script carries a default
# copy. IT IS YOURS TO CHANGE: edit this one string and re-run the render. The ten below are a
# starting point, not a rule; gov's own conf declares eight, having dropped `a11y` and `i18n` because
# it ships no user interface. Keep it on ONE line — a wrapped value reaches the retrieval corpus by
# its first row only.
READINESS_ROWS="security|perf / scale|a11y|i18n|error / empty / loading states|observability|risks|testing|migration|user docs"
# The date from which check 12 requires every declared row to appear in a Tier-2 spec's §5 body.
# Blank = never required; set it strictly ahead of your corpus as with the cutoffs above.
READINESS_ROWS_CUTOFF=""

# Base-resolve ratchet (check 12): from this date a LIVE spec must have the `base` sha in its status
# header resolve to a real commit here. Both tiers — `base` is a field both carry. Terminal specs are
# excluded, because a landed record is frozen. Every distinct sha goes through ONE
# `git cat-file --batch-check`, and the arm SKIPS itself with a stderr line in a shallow clone, where
# every commit outside the fetch depth answers `missing` and it could only red honest work. Blank =
# never required; set it strictly ahead of your corpus as with the cutoffs above.
BASE_RESOLVE_CUTOFF=""

# Section-7 leg-line ratchet, read by `tools/check-spec-tokens.py` rather than by the hygiene engine:
# from this date a LIVE spec that CARRIES a `## N. Gates` heading must contribute at least one leg
# name your gate manifest resolves. A spec with no Gates heading is silent, which is the whole Tier-1
# accommodation. Blank = never required. Declared here because a key that reaches only the shipping
# repo's conf leaves an adopter a dead arm reading as armed.
SPEC_LEGLINE_CUTOFF=""

# Fork-mark ratchet (check 12): at or after this date, a spec whose §8 Open questions carries any
# item must mark the SECTION with the documented resolution shape before its status may go terminal.
# Blank = never required. Set it ahead of your corpus at adoption, as with the two above.
FORK_MARK_CUTOFF=""

# Review-verdict ratchet (check 22): a review record whose FILENAME date is on or after this must
# open with one `## Verdict: <member>` line from the closed set. Blank = never required. Same
# grandfathering rule; the wrap-up derivation reads these verdicts, so a corpus without them makes
# that derivation resolve to nothing.
REVIEW_VERDICT_CUTOFF=""

# The document a session is pointed at before it touches code; check 16 derives the mandatory read
# path from it. Usually AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md.
CHARTER="AGENTS.md"

# --- corpus_ids.py (checks 13-15; check 16 is STRUCTURAL and behind no pin). Every number below
# --- is MEASURED against YOUR corpus by
# --- `python memory-tree/corpus_ids.py --measure`, and NEVER inherited from another repo: a pin
# --- copied from a larger tree is either vacuous or permanently red. Blank turns its check off.
# --- Ship them blank, run --measure once the tree has content, and paste what it prints.
# Orphan ids: cited but never defined. Shrink-only once set.
ORPHAN_ID_PIN=""
# Dead repo-path citations in the present-tense corpus. Measure 0 on a fresh tree and it becomes a
# ratchet: the next unresolving citation reds until it is registered or repaired.
DEAD_PATH_PIN=""
# READ_PATH_CEILING and READ_PATH_HEADROOM were RETIRED in memory-tree 2.42. Check 16 no longer
# sums the read path: check 6 already caps every member by class, so the sum was a second bound over
# an already-bounded population, and across 27 movements it never once caused a trim. A conf that
# still declares either key is ANNOUNCED on every run and reds nothing — delete the line when you
# see it. What survives is check 16's rules 3 and 4, which are STRUCTURAL and behind no pin.
# Check 6's per-class size caps. Declared here with the SHIPPED values rather than blank, because
# blank is not 'use the default' for these: the gate SOURCES this file, so an empty assignment
# overrides the built-in with an empty string and is refused at startup. Delete a line to take the
# default; change a number to take your own. A LINE cap of 0 means no independent line cap for that
# class, which is what the build-README tier ships with. A zero BYTE cap is refused: it would red
# every file in its class, which reads as a misconfiguration rather than an intent.
INDEX_CAP_BYTES="20480"         # a row document: an index a curation sweep prunes
INDEX_CAP_LINES="250"
GUIDE_CAP_BYTES="61440"         # a guide: prose the charter points a session at, read end to end
GUIDE_CAP_LINES="750"
BUILD_README_CAP_BYTES="25600"  # a build README: generated regions plus one bounded authored block
BUILD_README_CAP_LINES="0"
# Codebase-map dossiers, when that kit is adopted under this tree. Kept TIGHTER than the row cap on
# purpose: a dossier's remedy is a SPLIT rather than a rotation, and check 6 is the only size gate
# it has. MEASURE your own before raising any of these -- 250 rows at YOUR corpus's bytes-per-row is
# the derivation, and a figure copied from another tree is either vacuous or permanently red.
DOSSIER_CAP_BYTES="20480"
DOSSIER_CAP_LINES="0"
# Check 7's ENTRY budget: how long one line inside a capped index file may be, per class. Same
# refusal rules as the size caps above — not a whole number is refused, and a zero is refused
# because it reds every line rather than expressing an intent.
ENTRY_CAP_CHARS="300"           # a row document
BUILD_README_ENTRY_CAP_CHARS="350"  # a build README, whose authored rows were measured wider
# Path prefixes that are NOT repo CONTENT (e.g. a worktree root). Resolution is `git ls-files`
# membership plus a prefix scan and never touches the filesystem, so a checkout LOCATION classifies
# identically on every node — which is why the answer is declared here rather than probed.
DEAD_PATH_EXCLUDE=""
# Read-path members deliberately under no byte cap, space-separated. Blank = every member is capped.
READ_PATH_WAIVER=""

# gotchas.py (checks 17-19): how many bug-class records under $MEMORY_ROOT/gotchas/ may be
# `universal: true`. A universal record is emitted on EVERY reviewer checklist, so this is budgeted
# rather than left to drift — a checklist nobody can finish is not a checklist. Blank = no budget.
# Unlike the pins above this is a POLICY CEILING you choose, not a measurement of your corpus — so
# it ships with a value rather than blank. Raise it in a commit that says why.
UNIVERSAL_BUDGET="3"

# memory-recall only (safe to delete if that kit is not adopted): the cache budget over the whole
# `<git-common-dir>/recall/cache/` tree, in megabytes. ABSENT = the kit's default (512 MB);
# BLANK = uncapped.
RECALL_CACHE_BUDGET_MB="512"

# Check 20 — ids duplicated INSIDE one row document. A POLICY refusal, not a measured-blank
# pin: ship it with a value. `row_grammar.py --emit-pin` prints the measured count for a tree
# that already has content.
ROW_DUPLICATE_PIN="0"

# ACCEPTANCE_LEDGER_CUTOFF — the date from which a CLOSED Tier-2 spec owes an acceptance ledger: one
# line per numbered criterion, in a record whose `**Serves:**` kind is `journal`. The grammar is in
# HYGIENE.md under "Acceptance ledger", and the check reads SHAPE and COVERAGE only.
#
# BLANK GRANDFATHERS EVERYTHING AND THE CHECK RUNS DARK — set it to the date you adopt the grammar,
# not to a date in the past, or every spec that closed before it reds and the adoption is unlandable.
ACCEPTANCE_LEDGER_CUTOFF=""

# ACCEPTANCE_LEDGER_GRANDFATHER — a space-separated list of unit IDS, never patterns, exempted from
# the rule above. For the units that close across the cutoff date, where a date boundary falling
# mid-day is the whole of the reason. Naming them individually is what keeps the exemption auditable.
ACCEPTANCE_LEDGER_GRANDFATHER=""

# ---- FIVE VALUES A PROJECT OWNS — TOOL-dRetiredFork-15 -------------------------------------------
# Each of these was a literal inside check-memory-hygiene.sh, which is why an adopter with a
# different convention had to fork the checker to keep their own bar green. BLANK MEANS GOV'S
# CURRENT BEHAVIOUR for all five, so leaving this whole block untouched changes nothing.
#
# TWO OF THEM NARROW WHAT IS GRADED, and both are validated at startup: a bad value ABORTS rather
# than quietly grading nothing. Blank and invalid are different answers and the checker keeps them
# apart.

# BUILD_SLUG_RE — the pattern a build folder name must match. Blank = ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9-]*$
# MUST be anchored at both ends, and MUST NOT match the empty string: a pattern matching "" admits
# every folder name and check 4 then passes everything. The checker refuses all three cases.
BUILD_SLUG_RE=""

# PROJECT_REGISTRY_EXTRA — whitespace-separated extra filenames legal directly under
# <MEMORY_ROOT>/project/. This only WIDENS the whitelist and is bounded by what your tree actually
# contains, so it carries none of the silent-narrowing risk the two keys above and below do.
PROJECT_REGISTRY_EXTRA=""

# RECORD_SERVES_CUTOFF — an ISO date. Records whose filename date falls BEFORE it are exempt from
# check 21's "every record names the spec it is evidence about". For a tree adopting this kit
# mid-life with landed records that predate the grammar. A record whose name carries no parseable
# date is KEPT: unparseable is not the same answer as old, and only one of them is an exemption.
# A cutoff dated after today is REFUSED — it would exempt every record and report a clean run.
RECORD_SERVES_CUTOFF=""

# ENTRY_CAP_UNIT — `chars`, `bytes`, or blank. Blank leaves the counting exactly as your platform
# does it today, and that is deliberate. The checker's own comment explains why it pins no locale:
# doing so "would silently re-decide the cap on any adopter whose awk counts characters today". A
# DECLARED key re-decides nothing; it only makes the choice visible. Measured on gov's own node, the
# default counts BYTES, so `chars` is the value that changes anything there — which is precisely the
# platform-dependence this key exists to take out of the shadows.
ENTRY_CAP_UNIT=""
